  "body": "## What changed\n\nThe adaptive planner's own self-crossing check (`crates/motion/src/machining2d/adaptive/guard.rs`, the `scan` behind `first_crossing` / `crossing_pairs`) asks a new pair predicate, `crosses`, instead of the exact-sign `proper`.\n\n- `crosses` is the same proper-crossing (orientation) test, with one change. A chord end within `TOUCH` = **1e-9 mm** (1 pm) of the other chord's line counts as *on* it (sign 0), where only an exact zero did before. Two chords cross only when each one's ends stand strictly on opposite sides of the other's line. The test compares `|orient| <= TOUCH * |ab|`, with no division.\n- So a shared end point a rounding apart is read the way an exact shared vertex always was: a contact, not a crossing. That covers an orbit closing a ulp off its start (#980's seam), a microlift return landing on a chord end, and a ring starting on the last ring's vertex. A real crossing still crosses.\n- A non-finite point (NaN, +-inf) in any walk now reads as a crossing at that point (its walk paired with itself). Before, the length filter dropped NaN chords silently, and the bounds check looked only at x, so an infinite y slipped through.\n- `guard::proper` is unchanged: `lift_link` uses it for its own link law, which this PR leaves alone (#1080 touches it).\n- Only `guard.rs` changed, plus the new `guard_tests.rs` (pulled in with `#[path]`, so `mod.rs` is untouched), plus the decision file. No overlap with #953 or #1080.\n\n### The tolerance and why\nf64 rounding on a 10 m workspace is ~2e-12 mm per coordinate, so 1 pm sits about 1000x above it. Cutting tolerances are microns (the verifier's is 0.005 mm), about 1e6x above it. A chord end within 1 pm of a line can only meet the other chord at that end, so nothing a tool could cut hides inside it. The full reasoning is in `docs/decisions/1048-guard-shared-ends.md`.\n\n### Changed from the brief: contacts are not crossings\nThe first version excused only shared ends and treated T-touches and collinear overlaps as crossings. That failed the pinned `adaptive::tests::a_channel_is_grown_as_one_spiral_that_never_crosses`: in the lifted plan, pass 197 retraces pass 196's last chords exactly (32 chord pairs on the same line, same direction). The exact-sign law always read that as a contact (orient 0), and so does the verifier's law (a). A stricter check would send a good adaptive plan to Offset rings, the very loss this issue is about. The fix is therefore the old law with its exact zero widened to `TOUCH`. Whatever contact it accepted exactly, it now also accepts a rounding off. This is pinned and recorded in the decision file.\n\n## Tests (pins, `crates/motion/src/machining2d/adaptive/guard_tests.rs`)\n- `a_figure_eight_crosses_and_a_spiral_does_not` (moved from guard.rs, unchanged)\n- `a_spiral_returning_to_its_seed_within_rounding_does_not_cross`: jitters of 1e-15 and 1e-12 mm in 8 directions\n- `a_return_landing_on_a_chord_end_does_not_cross`\n- `a_ring_starting_on_the_previous_rings_vertex_does_not_cross`\n\n  Each of these three also asserts that the old exact-sign `proper` tripped on at least one of its jitters, so the knife edge is really exercised.\n- `a_real_crossing_near_a_shared_point_still_crosses`: a chord crossing 0.15 mm past a shared vertex, and a vertex a micron through the chord\n- `a_contact_is_no_crossing_and_a_micron_past_is`: the decided case. T-touches exactly on the chord, at ±1e-12 and at 5e-10 mm, a collinear overlap and a retraced chord are not crossings. A vertex 1e-6 mm past the chord is.\n- `a_non_finite_point_reads_as_a_crossing`: NaN and ±inf in x or y\n- `the_bucketed_scan_agrees_with_all_pairs`: 40 rounds of random walks that reuse each other's vertices 1e-12 apart, comparing the grid scan with the all-pairs predicate\n\n## Effect on the planner, goldens\nNo golden moved. `golden_cl` passes, and no golden file is in the diff. No fixture changed from Offset rings to adaptive or back in the suites run below. `a_channel_is_grown_as_one_spiral_that_never_crosses` still asserts `first_crossing(&pl.passes).is_none()` on its plan.\n\n## Gates (Linux x86_64, final head)\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main`: ok (motion lib + adaptive_band, adaptive_hsm, adaptive_orbit_seam, pocket3d_hsm), 1.1 min\n- By hand, debug: motion lib (197 passed), machining2d 66, rotary_adaptive 2, offset_shells 6, tiny_step 3, adaptive_hsm 1, adaptive_orbit_seam 1, pocket3d_hsm 3. App: golden_cl 25, conformance 6, shoulder_adaptive 3, roughing2d 7, whole_model_pocket 5, pocket3d_hsm 1, pick_agnostic 5. All green.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-motion --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean\n- Heavy pins (release `--ignored`): all green and none moved. motion adaptive_hsm (5, the M11.4 C metric report and the island budget pin included), motion pocket3d_hsm `the_stepped_block_holds_the_planner_laws_level_by_level`, motion rotary_adaptive (3), motion offset_shells (1), and app pocket3d_hsm `the_users_bore_roughs_adaptive_level_by_level_and_verifies_clean` (272 s; its pinned counts and verify-clean hold)\n\n## Not exercised\n- Windows and macOS (the predicate is plain f64 arithmetic, the same on every platform. The plan differences of #1010 come from elsewhere)\n- The UI and snapshot lanes (nothing in them is touched)\n\n## Left open\n- #1129 (filed): the verifier's law (a) (`sim/verify/laws.rs`, `proper_cross`) is still exact-sign. A plan with an end shared a rounding apart, which the planner now accepts, could still read as crossing there and hold the post. Still safe (refused by name), but the two laws should agree.\n\nChangelog: The Adaptive (HSM) planner no longer reads two walks that meet at one point, a rounding apart, as crossing itself, so such a level keeps its adaptive plan instead of falling back to Offset rings (#1048)\n\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nFork synced at v0.18.0\n\nFixes #1048\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
